ML_PAGE("l14-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l14\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 14 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 14 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 34 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>34</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>34</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l14\" data-quiz=\"14\">Start Chapter 14 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l14\" id=\"l14p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">34 practice questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14p-s4\" title=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\">Formula map and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l14\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 14 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 14 - Options: Puts and Calls - MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 50 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are illustrative practice suggestions, not official examination requirements. Choose one best answer before checking the separate key.</p>\n<p>Unless a question states otherwise, stock-option premiums, strike prices and stock prices are <strong>per share</strong>; each standard illustrative contract covers <strong>100 shares</strong>. Calculate profit at expiration, ignore commissions, taxes, financing and dividends, and assume options can be exercised under their stated terms. Contract multipliers and settlement procedures differ across products; always check the actual contract.</p>\n<p>Begin with <strong>position → right or obligation → underlying price relative to strike → payoff → premium → profit</strong>. At expiration, an option may have a positive payoff while the trade still loses money after the premium.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l14p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A call buyer holds which right?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To force the option writer to exercise the buyer's right.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To sell the underlying at the strike on demand.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To require the issuer to pay dividends to the option holder.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To buy the underlying at the strike under the option's terms.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A call gives its buyer the right to buy at the strike according to the contract.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A call buyer does not assume the writer's obligation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A put buyer holds which right?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To buy the underlying at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To sell the underlying at the strike under the option's terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To collect the underlying stock's dividend merely by owning the put.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To avoid paying the option premium.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A put gives its buyer the right to sell at the strike under the terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A put is not a right to buy.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A call writer is assigned on a physically settled option. What is the writer's obligation under the contract?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose whether the holder may exercise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy the underlying at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell the underlying at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pay dividends as the underlying company's shareholder.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An assigned call writer must sell the underlying if the contract is physically settled.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The writer cannot choose whether an exercising holder exercises; index contracts may settle differently.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A put writer is assigned on a physically settled option. What is the writer's obligation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy the underlying at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sell the underlying at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Decide whether the buyer exercises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Receive an additional premium at assignment.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An assigned put writer must buy the underlying at the strike under the physical-settlement example.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The premium received does not cancel the writer's obligation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A call has strike RM50. At expiration the stock is RM53. What is the call's payoff per share, before its premium?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM3.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM50.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM53.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Call payoff per share = $\\max(S_T-K,0)=\\max(53-50,0)=\\text{RM}3$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Payoff does not yet deduct the original premium.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>An investor buys one call with strike RM50 and premium RM5 per share. At expiration the stock is RM53. What is the investor's profit on the contract?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A gain of RM800.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A gain of RM300.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A loss of RM200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A loss of RM500.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Payoff is RM3 per share; profit = $(3-5)\\times100=\\text{-RM}200$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> In the money does not automatically mean profitable after premium.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>One long call has strike RM50, premium RM5 per share and an expiration stock price of RM75. What is the contract profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM2,500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM7,500.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Profit = $[\\max(75-50,0)-5]\\times100=\\text{RM}2{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The RM2,500 intrinsic payoff excludes the RM500 premium.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>One short call has strike RM50 and received premium RM5 per share. At expiration the stock is RM75. What is the writer's contract profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A gain of RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A loss of RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A loss of RM2,500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A gain of RM500.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Short-call profit = $[5-\\max(75-50,0)]\\times100=\\text{-RM}2{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Writer and buyer have opposite option profits on matched terms before costs, not the same sign.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A long put has strike RM50, premium RM5 per share and expiration stock price RM25. What is the profit on one contract?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A loss of RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM2,500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A loss of RM500.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Long-put profit = $[\\max(50-25,0)-5]\\times100=\\text{RM}2{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtract the premium from the RM2,500 payoff.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>The writer of the put in Q9 takes the opposite option position on identical terms. What is the writer's profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A loss of RM2,500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A gain of RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A loss of RM2,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A gain of RM500.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Short-put profit = $[5-\\max(50-25,0)]\\times100=\\text{-RM}2{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The writer receives premium but bears the exercise obligation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>An investor writes a call with strike RM50 and receives RM5 per share. The stock expires at RM25. Ignoring all other positions, the writer's contract profit is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A loss of RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM2,500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Zero because the option expired.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The call expires with zero payoff; the writer keeps $5\\times100=\\text{RM}500$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An expired option can produce a positive premium profit for its writer; slide 14-18's negative sign for the writer is an error.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A long call has strike RM50 and costs RM5 per share. Its expiration break-even stock price is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM50 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM45 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM55 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM500 per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Long-call break-even = $K+\\text{premium}=50+5=\\text{RM}55$ per share.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> RM500 is the contract premium, not the per-share increment to the strike.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A long put has strike RM50 and costs RM5 per share. Its expiration break-even stock price is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM45 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM55 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM50 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM5 per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Long-put break-even = $K-\\text{premium}=50-5=\\text{RM}45$ per share.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Put and call break-even move in opposite directions from the strike.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A call has strike RM50 when the stock is RM53. What is its moneyness?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> In the money by the premium, whatever its amount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Out of the money by RM3 per share in intrinsic value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> At the money and worth exactly RM3 under all conditions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> In the money by RM3 per share in intrinsic value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Call intrinsic value = $\\max(53-50,0)=\\text{RM}3$; the call is in the money.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intrinsic value does not establish trade profit or full pre-expiration market premium.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A put has strike RM50 when the stock is RM53. What is its intrinsic value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Negative RM3 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM3 per share; it is in the money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM50 per share; it is at the money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM0 per share; it is out of the money.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Put intrinsic value = $\\max(50-53,0)=0$, so it is out of the money.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intrinsic value is floored at zero, not negative.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A put has strike RM50, the stock trades at RM47 and the put premium is RM4.20 per share. What are intrinsic value and time value per share?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM3 intrinsic value and RM1.20 time value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM4.20 intrinsic value and no time value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM0 intrinsic value and RM4.20 time value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1.20 intrinsic value and RM3 time value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Intrinsic value = $50-47=\\text{RM}3$; time value = $4.20-3=\\text{RM}1.20$ per share.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Multiply by 100 only when converting a per-share amount to the contract amount.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A call is out of the money before expiration. Why might it still trade above zero?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It must already be profitable after its premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its intrinsic value must be negative.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its owner automatically receives the stock's dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It may have time value because the stock can move before the option expires.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>An out-of-the-money option can retain time value while a favorable move remains possible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Zero intrinsic value before expiration does not imply zero market premium.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A trader paid RM4 for a call and sells it for RM6 before expiration. What is the gain on one 100-share contract, ignoring costs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM400; the sale is impossible before expiration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM600; the original premium is irrelevant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM2; a contract always represents one share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM200; the holder sells the option and need not exercise it.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Closing gain = $(6-4)\\times100=\\text{RM}200$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Before expiry, selling the option can realize time value; exercise is not required to close a long position.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>In an illustrative case, 100 shares cost RM50 each and rise to RM75. What is the share investment's profit and simple return, ignoring costs and dividends?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2,500 profit and 5% return on RM50,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM2,500 profit and 50% return on RM5,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500 profit and 10% return on RM5,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM7,500 profit and 150% return on RM5,000.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Initial stock outlay = $100\\times50=\\text{RM}5{,}000$; profit = $100(75-50)=\\text{RM}2{,}500$; return = $2{,}500/5{,}000=50\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Slide 14-17 incorrectly states RM50,000 and 5% for the same quantities.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Why can a purchased option show a much larger percentage change than the underlying stock?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The option gives exposure to an underlying position for a smaller initial premium, so gains and losses relative to that premium can be magnified.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It guarantees a positive return whenever the stock moves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The holder automatically owns all 100 shares without exercising.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The premium cannot be lost.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A smaller premium investment can magnify percentage gains and losses compared with holding shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leverage magnifies losses as well as gains; the option can expire worthless.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which statement about maximum loss for an unhedged long call or long put is most accurate under the stated assumptions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is unlimited because the buyer must exercise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is limited to the premium paid for the option position.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It equals the underlying stock's entire market value in every case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is always zero if the option is in the money before expiry.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A stand-alone buyer's expiration loss cannot exceed the premium paid, under the stated assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Exercise or share ownership introduces additional exposure and is a different position.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Assuming a nonnegative stock price, what is the maximum loss on an unhedged short put with strike RM50 and premium RM5 per share at expiration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM5,000 plus the premium received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unlimited as the stock price falls below zero without bound.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500, the premium received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM4,500 per 100-share contract if the stock falls to zero.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>With stock at zero, loss = $(50-5)\\times100=\\text{RM}4{,}500$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An ordinary short put has substantial but bounded expiration loss if the underlying cannot fall below zero; the slide's claim that all naked options have unlimited loss is too broad.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>What is the potential expiration loss on an unhedged naked short call if the underlying stock can rise without limit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is theoretically unbounded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It cannot exceed the premium received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is bounded by the strike multiplied by the contract size.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is always zero if the call began out of the money.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A naked call writer's loss can grow as the stock price rises without limit, less the fixed premium received.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The premium is the maximum gain on the short call, not a cap on its loss.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM50 and writes one covered call at strike RM55, receiving RM2 per share. Ignoring dividends and costs, what is the maximum combined profit at expiration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM700.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Unlimited because the investor owns the shares.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Maximum combined gain = $[(55-50)+2]\\times100=\\text{RM}700$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The premium adds income, but the short call limits upside above the strike; the shares still face downside risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM50 and one protective put with strike RM45, paying RM2 per share. If the put is usable as specified, what is the maximum combined expiration loss assuming a nonnegative stock price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM700.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Unlimited.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>At or below RM45, combined proceeds are RM45 per share versus RM50 stock cost plus RM2 put premium: loss = $(50+2-45)\\times100=\\text{RM}700$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Protection costs money and lasts only under the option's specified terms.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>An investor shorts 100 shares at RM50 and buys one call with strike RM55 for RM3 per share. Ignoring stock borrowing costs and dividends, what is the maximum combined expiration loss?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unlimited if the call is held as specified.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM300.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM800.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The call caps stock-cover cost at RM55 per share; loss = $(55-50+3)\\times100=\\text{RM}800$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include the call premium and note the assumed short stock and call contract sizes match.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>An investor buys a RM50-strike call for RM4 and writes a RM55-strike call for RM2, both on the same stock and expiring at the same time. What are the maximum gain and maximum loss for this one-contract bull call spread?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Maximum gain RM200; maximum loss RM300.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Maximum gain RM500; maximum loss RM400.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Maximum gain RM300; maximum loss RM200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Unlimited gain and unlimited loss.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Net debit = $(4-2)\\times100=\\text{RM}200$; maximum spread payoff = $(55-50)\\times100=\\text{RM}500$; max gain = RM500 - RM200 = <strong>RM300</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Both options must share the stated underlying, expiration and multiplier.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>An investor buys a call for RM3 and a put for RM2, both with RM50 strike and the same expiration. What are the expiration break-even prices for this long straddle?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM47 and RM53 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM45 and RM55 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM48 and RM52 per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM50 only.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Total premium = RM3 + RM2 = RM5 per share; break-evens are $50-5=\\text{RM}45$ and $50+5=\\text{RM}55$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A long straddle needs a sufficiently large move to cover <em>both</em> premiums.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A cash-settled index call has strike 5,000, an expiration index level of 5,200 and a contract multiplier of RM100 per index point. Ignoring premium, its contract payoff is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM20,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM520,000.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Payoff = $\\max(5{,}200-5{,}000,0)\\times\\text{RM}100=\\text{RM}20{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The multiplier is RM100 per index point; the premium must be deducted separately to obtain profit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Which description of option markets and underlying assets is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Currency options reference exchange rates, index options reference an index, and LEAPS are longer-dated options; exercise style, settlement and expirations depend on the actual contract.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All listed options necessarily expire on the third Friday and all settle with 100 shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A currency option can never hedge foreign-exchange exposure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> LEAPS guarantee a longer-dated option buyer a profit.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Options can reference stocks, indexes, exchange rates and other underlyings; LEAPS are longer-dated options.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The 2014 slides' universal third-Friday expiration claim is historical/overgeneralized: inspect the actual contract's expiry, exercise style, settlement and multiplier.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>In the standard European option model, holding the stock price, strike, interest rate and time to expiration fixed, what is the general effect of a higher assumed stock-price volatility on a long call or long put's theoretical value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It lowers the value of both options because large movements are impossible.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It predicts with certainty that the stock will rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It generally increases both option values by increasing the range of possible favorable payoffs, without predicting direction.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the option's expiration date irrelevant.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>In the standard model, greater volatility generally raises the value of both calls and puts because favorable payoffs can expand while the long buyer's downside is limited to the premium.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Volatility describes dispersion, not a prediction that prices will rise; model values depend on assumptions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Which set lists the five principal inputs in the chapter's basic Black-Scholes model, under its simplifying no-dividend assumptions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Current stock price, dividend payout ratio, historical P/E, market capitalization and volume.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Stock price, bond coupon, book value, inventory turnover and strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Strike, tax rate, trading volume, EPS and intrinsic value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Current stock price, strike, time to expiration, risk-free rate and stock-price volatility.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The five inputs are stock price, strike, time to expiration, risk-free rate and volatility. The basic model assumes no dividends; extensions may incorporate dividends and other features.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A model estimate is not guaranteed to equal a tradable market price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>A quoted stock-option ask premium is RM2.40 per share. Ignoring fees, how much does a buyer pay for two contracts, each covering 100 shares, if the order fills at the quoted ask?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM480.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM240.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM24.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM4.80.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Cost = RM2.40 per share * 100 shares per contract * 2 contracts = <strong>RM480</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An ask is a quoted offer to sell; an actual fill at that price is assumed, and multipliers vary by contract.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>A European-style call has two months remaining and trades on a liquid secondary market. Which statement is correct about exercise and closing the position?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It can be exercised at any time before expiration, but cannot be sold.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is exercisable at expiration under its terms; the holder may nevertheless sell the option before expiration if a buyer and market are available.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot be exercised or sold at any time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its exercise style determines that its underlying must be a European stock.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>European-style options are exercisable at expiration, while an existing holder can generally close by selling before then if a suitable market exists.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Exercise style governs the exercise right, not whether a secondary-market closing sale is possible or the geographic location of the underlying.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l14p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula map and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l14_four_payoffs\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Expiry profit lines for the four positions (strike RM50, premium RM5). The buyer&#x27;s loss is capped at the premium; the call writer&#x27;s loss is unlimited, the put writer&#x27;s is large but bounded.</figcaption></figure></div><p>For one standard stock-option contract with multiplier $M=100$, per-share strike $K$, expiration stock price $S_T$, and per-share premium $p$:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long call profit: $[\\max(S_T-K,0)-p]M$; short call: $[p-\\max(S_T-K,0)]M$.</li>\n<li>Long put profit: $[\\max(K-S_T,0)-p]M$; short put: $[p-\\max(K-S_T,0)]M$.</li>\n<li>At expiration, long-call break-even = $K+p$; long-put break-even = $K-p$ when that price is feasible. Long-straddle break-evens use total call and put premiums.</li>\n<li>Before expiration, intrinsic value does not necessarily equal the option's market price: time value and other pricing factors matter. Closing a position by trading is different from exercise; contract terms and costs matter.</li>\n<li>A covered call holds stock plus a short call; a protective put holds stock plus a long put; a long call can cap the upward exposure of a matched short stock position. Include all position costs before judging combined profit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnose mistakes as rights versus obligations, call versus put direction, expiry payoff versus profit, per-share versus contract units, or incomplete hedge accounting. For each error, ask which underlying price would change the outcome.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original practice questions based on Chapter 14, <em>Options: Puts and Calls</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). The notes flag source arithmetic/sign errors and qualify generalizations about naked-option losses and expiration conventions. Examples use explicit hypothetical terms, not current contract specifications or investment recommendations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l14-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"14\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 14 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"14\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Both a call writer and a put writer are assigned on physically settled options. Which pairing of position and obligation is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Short call: must buy the underlying at the strike; short put: must sell it at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Short call: must sell the underlying at the strike; short put: must buy it at the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Short call: must sell the underlying at the strike; short put: must sell it at the strike as well.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Short call: may choose whether to deliver; short put: must buy the underlying at the strike.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A call gives its holder the right to buy, so an assigned call writer must sell at the strike; a put gives its holder the right to sell, so an assigned put writer must buy at the strike. A reverses the two. C gives the put writer the call writer's obligation. D is wrong because an assigned writer cannot choose: once the holder exercises, the writer must perform, and the premium received does not change that.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which statement defines an option's <strong>premium</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The strike price minus the share price at expiry, which is the payoff of a put held to expiry.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The amount by which the option is in the money, measured per share at the valuation date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The fee the writer pays the exchange to list the contract and to clear the trades made in it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The price the buyer pays the writer for the option, usually quoted per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The premium is the option's purchase price, quoted per share, so a quote of $\\text{RM}5$ on a $100$-share contract costs $5 \\times 100 = \\text{RM}500$. A and B describe payoff or intrinsic value, not the price paid. C confuses the premium with an exchange fee.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>A put has strike $K$ and the current share price is $S$. Which expression gives the put's <strong>intrinsic value</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\max(K - S,\\ 0)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\max(S - K,\\ 0)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $K - S$, which is negative when the put is out of the money</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $K - S + \\text{premium}$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Intrinsic value is what exercising would earn now, floored at zero: $\\max(K-S, 0)$ for a put. B is the call formula. C allows a negative value, but intrinsic value is never negative (the slide's \"neg.\" entries are the raw difference). D adds the premium, which belongs to profit, not intrinsic value.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the writer of an option receive a premium up front?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because writing an option transfers ownership of the shares to the buyer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the writer is guaranteed a profit equal to the premium, whatever the share price does and whether or not the holder exercises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because the holder may use the option whenever it benefits them, while the writer must perform if assigned.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the premium is a refundable deposit returned at expiry.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The contract is asymmetric: the buyer holds a right and the writer an obligation, and the premium pays the writer for accepting it. A is wrong because an option is not ownership. B is wrong because the writer can lose far more than the premium. D is wrong because the premium is a price, not a deposit.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>An American-style call with strike $\\text{RM}50$ is quoted at $\\text{RM}5.20$ when the share is $\\text{RM}53$. The holder wants out. Why might selling it be better than exercising today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The sale price includes $\\text{RM}2.20$ of time value that exercising would give up.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Calls cannot be exercised before expiry, so selling is the only option.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Selling cancels the writer's obligation and refunds the premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A call's market price can never exceed its intrinsic value, so both routes give the same cash.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Intrinsic value is $53 - 50 = \\text{RM}3$, so time value is $5.20 - 3 = \\text{RM}2.20$ per share. Exercising realizes only the $\\text{RM}3$; selling can capture the whole $\\text{RM}5.20$. B contradicts the stem, which says American style. C invents a refund. D is false: a market price above intrinsic value is exactly time value.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is the maximum expiry loss of a short put on a stock bounded, while that of a naked short call is not?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because put writers always own the shares they might have to buy, so they can never be forced to pay more than the strike.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because put premiums are always larger than call premiums.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because a put expires earlier than a call on the same stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because a share price cannot fall below zero but can rise without a theoretical limit.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A put's payoff is capped at the strike (the share can only fall to zero), so the writer's loss is at most $(K - p) \\times M$. A call's payoff grows as the share rises, with no ceiling. A, B and C are not true statements about puts and calls.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>An investor buys $2$ put contracts, each on $100$ shares, with strike $\\text{RM}60$ and premium $\\text{RM}3.50$ per share. At expiry the share is $\\text{RM}52$. What is the total profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}450$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}600$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}900$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}700$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Payoff per share $= 60 - 52 = \\text{RM}8$; profit per share $= 8 - 3.50 = \\text{RM}4.50$; for $200$ shares $= 4.50 \\times 200 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}900}$. A is one contract only. B ignores the premium ($8 \\times 200$). D is the premium alone ($3.50 \\times 200$).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A trader writes one call contract (100 shares) with strike $\\text{RM}35$ and receives $\\text{RM}1.80$ per share. At expiry the share is $\\text{RM}41$. What is the writer's profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $+\\text{RM}180$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-\\text{RM}420$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-\\text{RM}600$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $-\\text{RM}780$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The holder's payoff is $41 - 35 = \\text{RM}6$ per share, so the writer's profit is $(1.80 - 6) \\times 100 = \\mathbf{-\\text{RM}420}$. A counts only the premium. C ignores the premium received. D adds the premium to the loss instead of subtracting it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>An investor buys $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}80$ and a put with strike $\\text{RM}75$ for $\\text{RM}3$ per share, covering the same $100$ shares. Assuming the share price cannot be negative, what is the maximum expiry loss?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}800$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}500$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}300$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}7{,}500$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>At or below the strike the put offsets further stock losses, so the loss is $(80 - 75 + 3) \\times 100 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}800}$. B forgets the premium. C is the premium alone. D is the strike value of the shares, not the loss.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An investor buys a $\\text{RM}40$ call for $\\text{RM}7$ and writes a $\\text{RM}48$ call for $\\text{RM}3$ (same stock, expiry and multiplier). Which statement about one contract of this spread is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}48$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}400$; maximum loss $\\text{RM}700$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}44$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}400$; maximum loss $\\text{RM}400$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}44$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}800$; maximum loss $\\text{RM}400$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}47$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}400$; maximum loss unlimited.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Net debit $= 7 - 3 = \\text{RM}4$ per share. Maximum loss $= 4 \\times 100 = \\text{RM}400$. Maximum profit $= (48 - 40 - 4) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}400$. Break-even $= 40 + 4 = \\text{RM}44$. A uses the long call's premium alone. C forgets to subtract the debit from the strike gap. D has the wrong break-even and ignores that the written call is covered by the long call.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An investor buys $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}30$ and writes one call at strike $\\text{RM}33$ for $\\text{RM}1.20$ per share. The share ends at $\\text{RM}36$. Which breakdown of the result is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}720$: the full $\\text{RM}600$ share gain plus the $\\text{RM}120$ premium, because the call finished in the money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}600$: the premium is repaid to the holder on assignment, so only the share gain counts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}120$: only the premium counts once the call is exercised and the shares are called away.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}420$: share gain $\\text{RM}600$ plus premium $\\text{RM}120$ less the $\\text{RM}300$ call payoff.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The call holder's payoff is $(36 - 33) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}300$, which the writer pays. Total $= 600 + 120 - 300 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}420}$, equal to the maximum $(33 - 30 + 1.20) \\times 100$. A ignores the call obligation. B invents a premium refund. C drops the share gain up to the strike.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An investor buys a $\\text{RM}40$ call for $\\text{RM}2.50$ and a $\\text{RM}40$ put for $\\text{RM}1.50$ (a long straddle). The share ends at $\\text{RM}43$. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Profit $\\text{RM}300$, because the call is in the money by $\\text{RM}3$ and the put expires worthless at no further cost to the investor.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Profit $\\text{RM}50$, because the call gains $\\text{RM}3$ over the strike and only its own $\\text{RM}2.50$ premium has to be recovered.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss $\\text{RM}100$: the $\\text{RM}3$ payoff recovers only part of the $\\text{RM}4$ total premium (break-evens $\\text{RM}36$, $\\text{RM}44$).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss $\\text{RM}400$, because the put expires worthless and the call premium is lost.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Payoff $= \\max(43-40,0) + \\max(40-43,0) = \\text{RM}3$; total premium $= 2.50 + 1.50 = \\text{RM}4$; profit $= (3 - 4) \\times 100 = \\mathbf{-\\text{RM}100}$. Break-evens are $40 \\pm 4 = \\text{RM}36$ and $\\text{RM}44$, so $\\text{RM}43$ is still inside. A ignores the premiums. B deducts only the call premium, $(3 - 2.50) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}50$, and forgets the put's. D forgets the call's payoff.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor owns $100$ shares bought at $\\text{RM}50$. A $\\text{RM}45$ put costs $\\text{RM}2$; a $\\text{RM}55$ call can be written for $\\text{RM}2$. A colleague says \"both strategies protect the shares\". Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only the put sets a floor: at $\\text{RM}30$ the protective put loses $\\text{RM}700$ but the covered call loses $\\text{RM}1{,}800$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both set a floor at $\\text{RM}45$, because each uses the same strikes of $\\text{RM}45$ and $\\text{RM}55$ and each moves $\\text{RM}200$ of premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The covered call is the better protection because it brings in $\\text{RM}200$ while the put costs $\\text{RM}200$, so it leaves the investor ahead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither protects the shares, because exercise is always optional.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Protective put at $\\text{RM}30$: $-2{,}000 + 1{,}500 - 200 = -\\text{RM}700$, and this is the worst case. Covered call at $\\text{RM}30$: $-2{,}000 + 200 = -\\text{RM}1{,}800$; the premium is only a small cushion, and the written call also caps the upside. B and C confuse income with protection. D is wrong because the put's holder can exercise.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>The share is $\\text{RM}50$. A $\\text{RM}50$ call costs $\\text{RM}5$ and a $\\text{RM}60$ call costs $\\text{RM}1$. A friend argues the cheaper call is better because it earns a bigger percentage when the share jumps. Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: a lower premium always means a higher percentage return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incorrect: the $\\text{RM}50$ call is in the money, so it always beats the $\\text{RM}60$ call, in money and in percentage, at every expiry price, whatever the size of the move.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Partly: at $\\text{RM}70$ the cheap call returns $900\\%$ against $300\\%$, but at $\\text{RM}58$ it loses $100\\%$ while the other still makes $\\text{RM}300$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Irrelevant: both calls have identical percentage returns at every expiry price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>At $\\text{RM}70$: the $50$-strike call earns $(20 - 5) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}1{,}500$ on $\\text{RM}500$, or $300\\%$; the $60$-strike call earns $(10 - 1) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}900$ on $\\text{RM}100$, or $900\\%$. At $\\text{RM}58$ the $60$-strike call is worthless ($-100\\%$) while the other earns $(8 - 5) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}300$. A percentage in the best case ignores the likelihood of total loss. A, B and D overstate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A $\\text{RM}200{,}000$ portfolio of high-beta shares tracks an index at $2{,}000$ (its beta to the index is about $2$). The investor buys $10$ cash-settled index puts (strike $1{,}900$, premium $30$ points, multiplier $\\text{RM}10$ per point). The index falls to $1{,}800$, a fall of $10\\%$, and the portfolio falls $20\\%$. Evaluate the claim \"the puts fully protect the portfolio\".</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True: the put payoff of $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ offsets the whole loss, because the puts cover the same index level as the portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False: the portfolio loses $\\text{RM}40{,}000$, the puts pay $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ and cost $\\text{RM}3{,}000$, leaving a $\\text{RM}33{,}000$ loss, since it fell twice as much as the index.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> False only because of the premium: without the $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ cost the puts would hedge the portfolio perfectly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> True for any portfolio, since index options are cash-settled and need no delivery of the portfolio's shares.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Payoff $= 10 \\times (1{,}900 - 1{,}800) \\times 10 = \\text{RM}10{,}000$; premium $= 10 \\times 30 \\times 10 = \\text{RM}3{,}000$. Ending value $= 160{,}000 + 10{,}000 = \\text{RM}170{,}000$ against $203{,}000$ spent, a loss of $\\text{RM}33{,}000$. The shortfall comes from basis and composition risk plus the part of the index fall above the strike. C and D ignore that mismatch.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor owns $300$ shares bought at $\\text{RM}40$ and wants a design that limits the maximum expiry loss to at most $\\text{RM}1{,}500$, at the lowest premium cost. Which put purchase meets this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $2$ contracts, strike $\\text{RM}37$, premium $\\text{RM}1.20$ per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $3$ contracts, strike $\\text{RM}35$, premium $\\text{RM}0.50$ per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $3$ contracts, strike $\\text{RM}37$, premium $\\text{RM}1.20$ per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $3$ contracts, strike $\\text{RM}36$, premium $\\text{RM}0.80$ per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The max loss is $(S_0 - K + p) \\times 300$. D: $(4 + 0.80) \\times 300 = \\text{RM}1{,}440$ at a cost of $0.80 \\times 300 = \\text{RM}240$. C also qualifies ($\\text{RM}1{,}260$) but costs $\\text{RM}360$. B fails: $(5 + 0.50) \\times 300 = \\text{RM}1{,}650$. A covers only $200$ shares, leaving $100$ unprotected.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You want a bull call spread that buys the $\\text{RM}45$ call, has a net debit of $\\text{RM}3$ per share and a maximum profit of $\\text{RM}700$ per contract. Which strike should the written call have?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}55$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}52$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}58$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}48$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Maximum profit $= (K_2 - K_1 - D) \\times 100 = 700$, so $K_2 - 45 - 3 = 7$ and $K_2 = \\text{RM}55$. B gives $(52 - 45 - 3) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}400$; C gives $\\text{RM}1{,}000$; D gives $0$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A student designs a package: buy $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$, buy a $\\text{RM}45$ put for $\\text{RM}2$ and write a $\\text{RM}55$ call for $\\text{RM}2$ per share. What are the maximum profit and maximum loss at expiry?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Maximum profit $\\text{RM}700$; maximum loss $\\text{RM}700$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Maximum profit unlimited; maximum loss $\\text{RM}700$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Maximum profit $\\text{RM}500$; maximum loss $\\text{RM}500$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Maximum profit $\\text{RM}500$; maximum loss unlimited.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The two premiums cancel. At or above $\\text{RM}55$ the profit is $(55 - 50) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}500$; at or below $\\text{RM}45$ the loss is $(50 - 45) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}500$. The put sets the floor and the written call the cap. A is the protective put's loss with the covered call's gain. B ignores the cap and D ignores the floor.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>An option series shows $6{,}000$ contracts of volume for the day and an end-of-day open interest of $800$. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Impossible: volume can never exceed open interest, because every contract that trades stays outstanding afterwards and is counted there.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Possible: volume counts every trade in the period; open interest counts only contracts still outstanding, so same-day open-and-close trades add volume only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Possible only if every one of the $6{,}000$ contracts was an exercise rather than an ordinary trade, since exercises are never counted in volume at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Possible only for over-the-counter options, because listed options always report an open interest that is exactly equal to their volume.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Volume measures trading during a period; open interest counts outstanding contracts at the reporting point. A contract opened and then closed the same day is traded twice but leaves nothing outstanding. A, C and D are wrong, and neither number by itself guarantees liquidity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A broker's screen shows a call at bid $\\text{RM}2.10$ and ask $\\text{RM}2.30$. A client buys $5$ contracts (100 shares each) at the ask and, with the quotes unchanged, immediately sells them at the bid. What does the round trip cost, before fees?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}150$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}050$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}20$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}100$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Spread per share $= 2.30 - 2.10 = \\text{RM}0.20$; for $5$ contracts $= 0.20 \\times 100 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}100}$. A is the purchase cost and B the sale proceeds. C is the loss on a single contract. A buyer faces the ask and a seller the bid, so the spread is a real trading cost.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A $\\text{RM}2{,}400{,}000$ portfolio tracks an index at $3{,}000$. A hedger buys the $40$ cash-settled puts that match the portfolio (multiplier $\\text{RM}20$ per point), strike $2{,}850$, premium $45$ points. The index ends at $2{,}700$ and the portfolio falls $10\\%$. What is the profit relative to the initial portfolio plus the premium?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-\\text{RM}156{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-\\text{RM}240{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-\\text{RM}120{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $-\\text{RM}204{,}000$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Contracts $= \\dfrac{2{,}400{,}000}{3{,}000 \\times 20} = 40$. Premium $= 40 \\times 45 \\times 20 = \\text{RM}36{,}000$. Payoff $= 40 \\times (2{,}850 - 2{,}700) \\times 20 = \\text{RM}120{,}000$. Ending value $= 2{,}160{,}000 + 120{,}000 = \\text{RM}2{,}280{,}000$ against $2{,}436{,}000$: profit $-\\text{RM}156{,}000$. B is the unhedged loss, C omits the premium, and D takes the unhedged loss and treats the premium as income ($-240{,}000 + 36{,}000$) instead of a cost.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>An investor owns $100$ shares bought at $\\text{RM}40$. Position 1 writes a $\\text{RM}44$ call for $\\text{RM}1.50$. Position 2 buys a $\\text{RM}36$ put for $\\text{RM}1.00$. Which statement is correct at expiry?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Covered call break-even $\\text{RM}41.50$ and protective put break-even $\\text{RM}38.50$, because each premium is added to the purchase price, whatever kind of option it is.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Covered call maximum profit $\\text{RM}150$ and protective put maximum loss $\\text{RM}100$, since only the premiums count in each case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Covered call: break-even $\\text{RM}38.50$, maximum profit $\\text{RM}550$; protective put: break-even $\\text{RM}41$, maximum loss $\\text{RM}500$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both break even at $\\text{RM}40$ because both positions start from the same $100$ shares, whatever premium is paid or received.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Covered call: break-even $40 - 1.50 = \\text{RM}38.50$; maximum profit $(44 - 40 + 1.50) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}550$. Protective put: break-even $40 + 1.00 = \\text{RM}41$; maximum loss $(40 - 36 + 1.00) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}500$. A swaps and mis-signs the premiums. B uses premiums alone. D ignores the option premiums.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A Malaysian importer must pay $\\text{USD}50{,}000$ at expiry and fears the dollar will strengthen. It buys a cash-settled USD call with strike $\\text{RM}4.55$ per USD for a premium of $\\text{RM}0.06$ per USD, covering the full amount. At expiry the rate is $\\text{RM}4.80$. What is the importer's net ringgit cost?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}243{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}227{,}500$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}224{,}500$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}230{,}500$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Unhedged cost $= 50{,}000 \\times 4.80 = \\text{RM}240{,}000$. Call payoff $= 50{,}000 \\times (4.80 - 4.55) = \\text{RM}12{,}500$. Premium $= 50{,}000 \\times 0.06 = \\text{RM}3{,}000$. Net cost $= 240{,}000 - 12{,}500 + 3{,}000 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}230{,}500}$. A ignores the payoff, B ignores the premium, C subtracts the premium instead of adding it ($240{,}000 - 12{,}500 - 3{,}000$).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>An investor buys shares at $\\text{RM}40$ and writes a $\\text{RM}42$ call for $\\text{RM}1.50$. The shares pay a $\\text{RM}0.80$ dividend before expiry, and the investor still holds the shares and is not assigned early. Which pair is correct (per share, before other costs)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}37.70$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}4.30$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}38.50$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}3.50$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}37.70$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}3.50$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Break-even $\\text{RM}42.30$; maximum profit $\\text{RM}4.30$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The dividend and the premium both reduce the cost basis: break-even $= 40 - 1.50 - 0.80 = \\text{RM}37.70$. Maximum profit $= (42 - 40) + 1.50 + 0.80 = \\text{RM}4.30$. B ignores the dividend. C applies the dividend to one measure only. D adds the dividend and premium instead of subtracting them. This follows the notes' treatment of a dividend in the covered-call table.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An investor buys shares at $S_0 = \\text{RM}50$ and a put with strike $\\text{RM}50$ for $\\text{RM}4$ (a protective put with $K = S_0$). Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The profit equals that of a short call with strike $\\text{RM}50$, because the put offsets the shares exactly at every price, making the two positions mirror images.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The profit equals that of the long put alone, because the shares add nothing when the strike equals the purchase price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The profit equals that of a long call only when $S_T$ is below $\\text{RM}50$, and differs from it above that price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The profit equals that of a long $\\text{RM}50$-strike call bought for $\\text{RM}4$, since $S_T + \\max(50 - S_T, 0) = 50 + \\max(S_T - 50, 0)$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Profit $= M[(S_T - 50) + \\max(50 - S_T, 0) - 4] = M[\\max(S_T - 50, 0) - 4]$. At $S_T = 58$: $(8 + 0 - 4) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}400$; at $S_T = 44$: $(-6 + 6 - 4) \\times 100 = -\\text{RM}400$, the same as the call. A has the wrong sign, B omits the shares, C restricts a relationship that holds for every price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A client holds $10{,}000$ shares worth $\\text{RM}20$ each and cannot afford to lose more than $\\text{RM}30{,}000$ from today's value over the next quarter. A $\\text{RM}18$ put costs $\\text{RM}0.70$; a $\\text{RM}22$ call can be written for $\\text{RM}0.60$. Which recommendation is best supported?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Write the call: its $\\text{RM}6{,}000$ of income more than covers any fall in the share price over the quarter, so the limit is met.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy the put: the worst loss from today's value is $(20 - 18 + 0.70) \\times 10{,}000 = \\text{RM}27{,}000$, inside the limit; the covered call could lose $\\text{RM}54{,}000$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reject the put: the loss would be $\\text{RM}27{,}000$ plus the $\\text{RM}7{,}000$ premium, which breaches the $\\text{RM}30{,}000$ limit, so the client should sell or write calls instead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both meet the limit, because each option's premium offsets part of any fall in the share price over the quarter.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The protective put's floor gives a maximum loss of $\\text{RM}27{,}000$ with the premium already included. The covered call at $\\text{RM}14$ loses $(14 - 20 + 0.60) \\times 10{,}000 = -\\text{RM}54{,}000$, so it does not meet the need. C double counts the premium, which is already inside the $\\text{RM}2.70$ per share. D treats a small cushion as protection. Protection also ends at expiry, which the advice should state.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A trader says: \"A short put at strike $\\text{RM}50$ for $\\text{RM}5$ is safer than owning the shares at $\\text{RM}50$, because its loss is bounded.\" Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: the short put's loss is bounded by its premium and strike, while the loss on the shares is not bounded at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct: the short put loses nothing unless it is exercised, and exercise is rare for an option sold out of the money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect: both losses are bounded; the short put can lose $\\text{RM}4{,}500$ against $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ for the shares, and its gain is capped at $\\text{RM}500$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect: the short put's loss is unlimited, exactly like a naked call, because the writer must buy at the strike whatever happens.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With a nonnegative share price both positions have bounded losses. Short put: $(50 - 5) \\times 100 = \\text{RM}4{,}500$. Shares: $50 \\times 100 = \\text{RM}5{,}000$. The put writer gives up the upside beyond the $\\text{RM}500$ premium. A wrongly says the stock's loss is unbounded. B is not a risk measure. D contradicts the notes.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Which set of corrections to the slides is fully valid?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ cost $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ and return $5\\%$ on a $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ gain; a writer of a worthless call has $-\\text{RM}500$; all naked options have unlimited loss.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ cost $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ and a $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ gain is $50\\%$; a worthless call gives its writer $+\\text{RM}500$; a short put on a nonnegative stock has a bounded loss, unlike a naked call.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ cost $\\text{RM}50{,}000$ and return $5\\%$; a worthless call gives its writer $+\\text{RM}500$; all naked options, including a short put on a stock, have unlimited loss.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ cost $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ and a $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ gain is $50\\%$; a worthless call costs its writer $\\text{RM}500$; a short put on a stock has an unbounded loss.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Slide 14-17 mis-multiplies ($100 \\times 50 = 5{,}000$, so $2{,}500 / 5{,}000 = 50\\%$). Slide 14-18 has the writer's sign wrong: the premium received is $+\\text{RM}500$. Slide 14-41's blanket claim is too broad: a short put is bounded. Each other option keeps at least one slide error.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A researcher records $500$ long straddles and finds that $55\\%$ ended beyond an expiry break-even. She concludes that straddles are underpriced. What is the main flaw?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Break-even frequency ignores the size of gains and losses, premiums that already reflect implied volatility, and costs; average profit and a model comparison are needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A straddle cannot lose money because one of its two options is always in the money, so no empirical study of outcomes or of premiums is needed at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The study is valid: any outcome beyond break-even is a profit, so more than half of outcomes being profitable proves that straddles are underpriced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Straddles must be tested using puts only, because a call can be valued at just one strike and expiry, so the call leg adds nothing to a test.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A high hit rate can coexist with a negative average profit if the losses at the strike (both premiums) exceed the gains beyond break-even. The notes warn that expensive volatility is already in the premium, that a model price is conditional, and that costs matter. B is false: the worst case is the full premium. C confuses frequency with expected profit. D is irrelevant.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Design a test of how well index puts hedge a portfolio that is not the index. Which design is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Compare the option's payoff at the strike with the portfolio's loss in one scenario of a $10\\%$ index fall, and treat the premium as a sunk cost that need not be counted in the comparison.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Report only the premium as a percentage of portfolio value, since a cheaper hedge is always a better hedge for any portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Assume the portfolio's return equals the index's, since both are diversified, and report the put payoff less the premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tabulate many index outcomes, model the portfolio's own change given the index, net the put payoff and premium, and report the residual loss and the region above the strike.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The notes stress that a portfolio that differs from the index keeps basis and composition risk, that the put protects only below the strike, and that the premium is a cost. D measures all three over a range of outcomes. A uses a single scenario. B measures cost, not effectiveness. C assumes away the very risk being tested.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
